WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.368
Hashing appears in many C++ systems, but it does not always mean the same thing.

2
00:00:05.368 --> 00:00:13.957
A hash may identify a file, verify that downloaded content has not changed, store a password verifier, support a signature workflow, create a cache

3
00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:16.820
key, compare structured data, or protect integrity metadata.

4
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:23.620
Those use cases sound related because they all produce a digest, yet their security expectations can be very different.

5
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.914
The first defensive habit is to separate ordinary hashing from cryptographic hashing.

6
00:00:27.914 --> 00:00:35.430
A fast hash for an unordered container or cache lookup is useful, but it is not designed to protect security decisions.

7
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:44.377
A cryptographic hash can be useful for a file fingerprint, but it is still not the right answer for password storage or keyed message authentication.

8
00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.166
Hashing is also not encryption.

9
00:00:46.166 --> 00:00:50.818
A digest is not meant to be decrypted back into the original input.

10
00:00:50.818 --> 00:00:52.608
Secure hashing starts with purpose.

11
00:00:52.608 --> 00:01:01.197
Before choosing an algorithm, a library, an output length, or a storage format, the team should know what the digest is expected to prove.

12
00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:07.996
Is it a fingerprint, a password verifier, an authenticated integrity check, a compatibility marker, or a non-security identifier?

13
00:01:07.996 --> 00:01:11.933
The answer affects error handling, storage, comparison, migration, and incident response.

14
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:20.164
Once the purpose is clear, C++ code can use approved libraries, documented wrappers, and reviewable formats instead of treating hashing as a generic

15
00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:20.880
utility call.
